{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2197-05-29 11:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I’ll be taking care of you today. Can you tell me what brought you to the hospital?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2197-05-29 11:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, I’ve been having really bad nausea and vomiting all morning. It’s like my usual gastroparesis flare, but worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2197-05-29 11:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Any abdominal pain, fever, or diarrhea?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2197-05-29 11:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, no pain or fever. But I did throw up a little blood when I got here. That scared me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2197-05-29 11:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s important. We’ll keep an eye on that. You also mentioned chills—any other new symptoms?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2197-05-29 11:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Just the chills since I’ve been here. Otherwise, same as usual—just can’t keep anything down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2197-05-29 11:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. We gave you Zofran and Ativan in the ER for nausea. After the Ativan, your breathing slowed and oxygen dropped, so we moved you to ICU for monitoring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2197-05-29 11:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Wait—was that because of the Ativan? I’ve taken it before without problems."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2197-05-29 11:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it was likely the 1 mg IV dose. You usually take 0.25 mg by mouth, right? Higher IV doses can affect breathing, especially if you're already unwell."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2197-05-29 11:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, okay. So I should avoid higher doses then?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2197-05-29 11:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. We’ll make a note in your chart. You’re breathing fine now, which is great."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2197-05-29 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your labs came back. Blood sugar was high—302 this morning. We’re watching for signs of DKA, but you’re not in it. We adjusted your insulin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2197-05-29 14:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My sugars have been running high lately. Did we figure out why?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2197-05-29 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s likely due to vomiting—you can’t keep food or meds down, so insulin balance gets thrown off. We’re giving you IV fluids and adjusting your coverage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2197-05-29 14:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Makes sense. What about the blood I vomited?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2197-05-29 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We think it’s from a small tear in the esophagus—called a Mallory-Weiss tear—caused by forceful vomiting. Your hemoglobin is stable, so no active bleeding."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2197-05-29 14:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, okay. Should I be worried?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2197-05-29 14:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not right now. We’ve stopped your aspirin temporarily and started a stronger stomach protector—Protonix twice daily for six weeks."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2197-05-29 14:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Thanks."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2197-05-30 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your blood pressure spiked last night—up to 215/140. That’s called hypertensive urgency. We gave IV meds to bring it down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2197-05-30 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Was that because of the vomiting or stress?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2197-05-30 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Likely both, and you couldn’t take your lisinopril by mouth. We restarted it today now that you’re tolerating liquids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2197-05-30 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. Are my sugars better?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2197-05-30 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, they’ve come down with adjusted insulin. We’ll keep monitoring and get you back to your home regimen before discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2197-05-30 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve added IV erythromycin. It helps with stomach emptying and might be useful for your gastroparesis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2197-05-30 15:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve never taken that before. Is it safe with my other meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2197-05-30 15:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ve checked. It’s a short course. We’ll stop it when you leave, but you should discuss continuing it with your GI doctor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2197-05-31 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing better. You’ve kept down clear liquids and your nausea is less. We’re going to try small amounts of soft food today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2197-05-31 08:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. I’m hungry, but I’m nervous about vomiting again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2197-05-31 08:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Totally understandable. We’ll go slow—small bites, frequent checks. If you keep it down, we’ll advance further."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2197-05-31 13:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great job—you tolerated applesauce and crackers. We’re upgrading to a soft diet."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2197-05-31 13:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. What about my gastric pacer? It hasn’t been helping as much lately."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2197-05-31 13:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good point. Dr. ___ is back next week. We’ll schedule a pacer check to see if it needs adjustment."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2197-06-01 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your blood pressure is still a bit high, but more stable. We used hydralazine for peaks, but now it’s mostly controlled with your usual meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2197-06-01 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Should I worry about my heart? My dad died of heart disease."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2197-06-01 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We checked your EKG—it shows sinus tachycardia and some changes from high BP, but no signs of heart attack. Controlling your pressure helps reduce long-term risk."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2197-06-01 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thanks. That helps."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2197-06-01 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your chest X-ray and abdominal film were normal. The pacer is in place, and no signs of blockage or free air."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2197-06-01 15:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good to know. Any other tests?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2197-06-01 15:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We did a MRSA screen—negative, so no infection concern. Main issues remain gastroparesis, blood pressure, and blood sugar control."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2197-06-02 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve made great progress. You’re on a regular diet, no vomiting, and labs are stable. We’re planning for discharge tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2197-06-02 08:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great! What meds will I go home on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2197-06-02 08:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Same as before: Reglan, lisinopril, clonidine, Protonix, and insulin. We’re adding a low-dose lorazepam for nausea—only half a 0.5 mg pill if needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2197-06-02 08:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So no more Ativan?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2197-06-02 08:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You can take the lorazepam—it’s the same drug family—but only half your usual dose. Avoid anything higher due to your breathing reaction."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2197-06-02 08:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. And the erythromycin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2197-06-02 08:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re stopping it here, but bring it up with your GI doctor. It seemed to help, so they might want to continue it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2197-06-03 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "All set for discharge. You’re stable, eating well, and symptoms are controlled."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2197-06-03 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. Any warning signs I should watch for?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2197-06-03 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes—call if nausea or vomiting returns, or if you see blood in vomit or stool. Also watch for high blood sugar, severe headache, or trouble breathing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2197-06-03 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And follow-up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2197-06-03 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "See your GI doctor soon to discuss the pacer and erythromycin. Also follow up with your endocrinologist about insulin management."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2197-06-03 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks for everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2197-06-03 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and don’t hesitate to come back if needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2197-06-03 10:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll make sure all my doctors know about the Ativan reaction."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2197-06-03 10:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. That’s very important for your safety."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2197-06-03 10:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And I’ll advance my diet slowly at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2197-06-03 10:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Small meals, low fat, low fiber. Avoid heavy or greasy foods that can trigger symptoms."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2197-06-03 10:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Thanks again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2197-06-03 10:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing great. We’re discharging you home in stable condition. Take it easy and follow up soon."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2197-05-29 10:49:00",
    "admission_end": "2197-06-03 13:18:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 30-year-old woman with Type 1 diabetes, gastroparesis s/p gastric pacer, and hypertension was admitted for intractable nausea and vomiting. She developed respiratory depression after receiving 1 mg IV Ativan in the ED, requiring ICU monitoring, but recovered within 24 hours. She also experienced hypertensive urgency with BP up to 215/140, managed with IV labetalol and hydralazine, and presented with a single episode of hematemesis likely due to a Mallory-Weiss tear, prompting temporary discontinuation of aspirin and initiation of twice-daily PPI therapy. Her blood glucose was elevated on admission, raising concern for DKA, but was managed with increased insulin coverage without needing an insulin drip. She was treated with IV fluids, antiemetics including ondansetron, Reglan, and IV erythromycin, and advanced to a regular diet. She was discharged home in stable condition tolerating oral intake, with instructions to avoid high-dose benzodiazepines, follow up with GI for pacer interrogation and erythromycin evaluation, and continue home medications including adjusted lisinopril and insulin regimen.",
    "problems": [
      "Diabetes with neurological manifestations, type I [juvenile type], uncontrolled",
      "Hematemesis",
      "Malignant essential hypertension",
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Persistent vomiting",
      "Respiratory abnormality, unspecified",
      "Esophagitis, unspecified",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Benzodiazepine-based tranquilizers causing adverse effects in therapeutic use"
    ]
  }
}
